#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199277} :activity/id #uuid "4228fb68-d9aa-54a5-8d69-9202b473f5c5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321 :topic/id #uuid "ebd1a7e8-239e-581a-aa6c-f96c639be509" :topic/math-academy-id 4135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198011 :question/id #uuid "3b27595a-5d6d-5ea3-92d2-6de7895c19fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-11153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050087}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423500}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "9c75dcfe-d31b-552f-8b7f-bb0e2c83fbe1" :step/math-academy-id 42408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199275 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198017 :question/id #uuid "b9d1a634-f1ef-5130-83ca-f3a555027699" :question/math-academy-id "e-12332"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442759}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "8bab0285-041c-5645-bfb0-d1af1dccc616" :step/math-academy-id 44184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199463 :tutorial/content "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nA sketch of $\\Delta$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n     \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ onto the $xy$-plane, we need to transform each side (bottom, top, left, and right) of $\\Delta$ separately: \n\n![](images/06/062a3d1f13e8566770118650c92231ebe17f5eba2b4ae8d8b41722e33a72baff.png)\n\nIt can be shown that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$ So, let's proceed to find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by mapping these four sides." :tutorial/id #uuid "89fbd7db-7e0a-5047-9be9-1a886c8970f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7615 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e182722-189e-524f-a581-82e71805ddc4" :step/math-academy-id 44217 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198019 :question/id #uuid "4eaf3262-b6e3-57bc-9662-42ab52626ee9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423502}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "c6da0258-e96c-5bbb-91d8-5c5aab571523" :step/math-academy-id 44222 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199464 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following transformation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v), \\qquad u > 0, \\quad 0\\leq v < 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nIf we substitute $u = r$ and $v = \\theta,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(r,\\theta) \\to (r \\cos \\theta, \\, r \\sin \\theta), \\qquad r > 0, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta < 2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that these are the usual formulas that we use to write the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta)$ of a point in the plane in Cartesian $(x,y)$ coordinates. \n\nThis gives us a fresh perspective of the meaning behind the usual $(r,\\theta)\\to(x,y)$ formulas:\n\n- Firstly, they describe a transformation that maps a region $\\Delta$ in the $(r,\\theta)$ plane (often a rectangle) to another region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Similarly, the inverse transformation (which always exists) maps a region $D$ in the $xy$-plane to a region $\\Delta$ in the $(r,\\theta)$ plane.\n\nThis idea becomes important later on. But, broadly speaking, many problems become easier after transforming them to another coordinate system where the domain under consideration is rectangular." :tutorial/id #uuid "49541498-bef0-5f43-a3fc-95ac9db70e7e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7618 :tutorial/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "c28b3ebd-437b-5803-87ec-1e04e0f5c191" :step/math-academy-id 44223 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199275}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198026 :question/id #uuid "f4539253-b127-5d96-a331-3b357dc08a9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442760}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "616ce947-0b5d-5274-955e-7ae934283b51" :step/math-academy-id 44308 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199475 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8be5595b-5342-5f9e-9e69-9591c32d7780" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7670 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment"} :step/id #uuid "2349a1aa-a4dc-5683-a96c-be82f8b7ab2e" :step/math-academy-id 44555 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199309}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199476 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "74b0f131-a207-5518-ade5-32ceacbf2263" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7671 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment"} :step/id #uuid "21530539-0f1c-512e-92ce-ac7f86f8be41" :step/math-academy-id 44556 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199267}}] :activity/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198481}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442762} :activity/id #uuid "5c82b827-3e4e-5f9f-9368-26da9d8bf53c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049321 :topic/id #uuid "ebd1a7e8-239e-581a-aa6c-f96c639be509" :topic/math-academy-id 4135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdd1c39-66d8-5f61-b299-ab1278eef22f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/13810a874a27ea2db773ed90fa02f5b1b833d90372b38a3273f416b30d9a1a1f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275770} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1db914-4a3b-567d-9004-9871a995b025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d3/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276003} {:answer/id #uuid "46942c75-8495-50b8-9294-5fc54ade1f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276102} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2e281c-1956-541a-acc6-967b6de81c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/de/deafbae15e6d30f175122d3662397f05398d31579b80520648accaf1fff93e85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276317} {:answer/id #uuid "81a53031-c557-57d2-8e30-24920e8620f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cb/cbcba98cd11ce740be941848d0d9d4524c8cbc58d6de7aee0cfb2f7421c457db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276003} :answer-field/id #uuid "56af4b0d-dbe5-5968-a3ec-fbb544ad8284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a3b231b2-8f67-5351-80e4-0c74d7566eae" :question/math-academy-id "q-161026" :question/problem "![](images/70/708bf4df2d45383b0dd367f5bfe3dc9e260a17cfefe0566e2b6d37dec98af2ef.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $0\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})),\\,0\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d329b641155ad693c515ed4d8229f8bd7dbd9d0aab8442e9f65391316d5aa7a3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "3e559c32-32ad-5b51-a713-cef4abf5f5ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442762 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121a9eee-f06d-5c31-b803-574ca8ff0d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/39ba02dcd51d54bf28a84e19786d763c201858979c45f65a11069b7ba4d0584d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275641} {:answer/id #uuid "236695f1-93f1-5355-8854-dd3673ec110b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/0799c923b5e7061f8a6ec1faf9ed8b73038dfe4c1b63607a8e037d66d6ce88fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275791} {:answer/id #uuid "4e728a54-7547-5708-838f-f3754fc2ad67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/25/25ec9c36f1e6637a0034cdae142977b4a4b5c3237471ef93f4bf55c6f4ea86b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276172} {:answer/id #uuid "6589c629-8015-54c0-992a-6de54e9fc089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/52/5263c0c82be5ab6b0290e5017943c7c2584d3bd23783c5420e38af2df618f9b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276388} {:answer/id #uuid "88b3cf04-1b74-59c1-8925-34f0ae5cd098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5adf324eba50123bd9a6dc3f27be38a7db0b4835bac8479e705f696abcf404b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275641} :answer-field/id #uuid "685aec8b-f5ad-57e6-a7ef-205ccbdc098d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "105c355c-8c9b-5cb0-b7b0-305140e2d0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-160914" :question/problem "![](images/36/3621e70552c3891f43521e74704d5db3f276f9928669390d688cca1bc3449217.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\frac{6\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c8/c8ad4cf64e9ea17b225373d8ed238c8588c0b6f45577d2ca15b22ede5e34d7d8.png)"} :step/id #uuid "64d08072-1a49-5e0c-9af1-0ecfb9d8073a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d20667f-50b2-55b0-95fa-20f52b560b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442747} {:answer/id #uuid "9d738677-4b48-5272-ba62-6f070fe8babb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442751} {:answer/id #uuid "a8188bf8-8b54-575b-933a-aa859ce24d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442752} {:answer/id #uuid "d5079a63-2c21-5532-8a24-134a5b4af955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442753} {:answer/id #uuid "de7d22b9-d57c-5d7b-88cb-f0b4ca5f4a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442755} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cd74140-69a0-5af3-8672-f40c3db56533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6caf644a-b965-5b35-b048-eee4060c43c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161034" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3bef2dbb84cfe3a2881abf8f61761f98ea0d9cf4a07ea582355c57d375f975d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $3$ and $6$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $3\\le u\\le 6.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})),\\,3\\le u\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b976e62862ba82dfd89ff28627239dd5b5d1be1144e257223c091f29a5729f89.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=3,$ we obtain the point \n$(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $3.$\n\n- When $u=6,$ we obtain the point \n$(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $6.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $3$ and $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9b9a8694-189b-58cf-bf5d-4a0e9eed87f4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87b9e5b4-dff5-5eec-ba80-fedc99f32b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f3/f3425325c560606e15fc752f18e637f7ccab0eec8c973f022a4976b8477a7492.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442748} {:answer/id #uuid "8c755e5e-6783-571f-a88c-164c24a1dfcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/40/40d60714c368f92bd7382005882c627be119f6cb05665441730887b60306cdb7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442749} {:answer/id #uuid "98deceaf-7a7c-5395-80de-66909b2600ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f0/f0419b0c1195c443579bc12cf5ab8abd4b0d115ddb6f9ac24f9cf367fb82c2b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442750} {:answer/id #uuid "da76ff9a-9c3b-5dcc-86dd-ebe90f5c27fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/71/716d1dc2aee5a523e5a9619fdf166f2a9fccd02475212752baea44a26a9db47f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442754} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bfc4f2-05c7-5141-aec8-57ca2deb933f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fee321af3b544d653c22ff2c7a573dd1c8cd3c93bf42bd65d592f7264a49de74.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442749} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fe8e38-4aad-57a4-8a8d-5a0d162037eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57a07fec-924e-51b2-9054-16b08d5b6be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-160949" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b0365b0f6b00859fb3739876f7d9a119db8b7f649e1935c66091285460e7f70.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=1,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/40/40d60714c368f92bd7382005882c627be119f6cb05665441730887b60306cdb7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d73744fd-8d46-5808-92e1-0ec7ee597069" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e49242-5f05-594e-879e-d014e1481c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/81/81d08b5c3f511d6a51c1fc01d40ef25cf52b1fc8f10c055f924425aab7f9e39c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389155} {:answer/id #uuid "5d15ed38-8317-574a-8f57-c7ef6444372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8a/8a8b84b7858341f49fdba9e64c13f0544cce74c8f8c6128527f9b42e9c3bb718.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389156} {:answer/id #uuid "81c25f98-84c7-57e9-bb8b-9cd6e1af76e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/89/89f8cd5fdb19ae5a3eb74bb1d519da855ea6f32027aa3e536c8cea99228c13cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389157} {:answer/id #uuid "186ec206-6c76-5992-b7ad-40bb2541f704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/88/88d58291666ed60113e3d16c5bc30e6d4375cbae3501f8e3e26bd9bbacb4940d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389158} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ab65d8-b72e-5eee-a7fe-4d1446facdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8f/8f0573ca9a95fc277d1993e08b7307d2a9e8be48b80e1ea2360f9fc688b440b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389158} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f30debe-a057-56e2-b225-26c5a2263926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "087f8af8-ce2b-5e52-94ce-b9f44a5bd078" :question/math-academy-id "q-161116" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9cd25963ec1ae28860b821825597ca16074e3b0231bd83da7505d37405c23c2.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/88/88d58291666ed60113e3d16c5bc30e6d4375cbae3501f8e3e26bd9bbacb4940d.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d7f0238c-5d88-53fd-9153-3c953fcbb734"}] :activity/title "Polar Coordinate Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442746}]}
